[#:seon.task{:name "the-queue-on-an-agent-branch-offers-tasks-claimed-on-the-cluster"
             :kind :function
             :intent "After (`my.task/claim!` {`:seon.task/assigned` \"cb07d384b078\"}) by agent e1-probe on its branch (2026-09-28), (`my.task/open`) on that branch still offers cb07d384b078: claim! writes the assignment to the task home (`seon.task/home-connection`), while open reads the branch's database, whose copy of the task has no assignment. One queue for every agent means open reads where claims are written. The same call costs 3,529 armed calls for 7 tasks (`seon.db/database-value?` 774x). Done when a claimed task leaves the queue read from the claimant's branch, with a regression."
             :must-read #{my.task/open my.task/claim! seon.task/home-connection}
             :evidence #{"tmp/health-loop-proof/evidence.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "no-per-function-health-view-and-open-misses-over-bound-tasks"
             :kind :function
             :intent "There is no per-function health view (its tasks and faults on one function), and `my.task/open` misses over-bound function tasks. `my.task/open` also repeats fault resolution once per task that shares a signature (D30) and answers about 954 tokens. Done when one call answers a function's tasks, open faults and over-bound warnings together, `my.task/open` includes over-bound function tasks, resolves each shared fault signature once, and its token size and time are asserted."
             :must-read #{my.task/open seon.task/open-tasks}
             :evidence #{"tmp/repl-audit/read.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "task-creation-is-not-an-agent-call"
             :kind :function
             :intent "my.task (the agent-facing API) exposes open, plan!, step! and done! but not task creation: `seon.task/create!` (task.clj:353) creates a runtime task from a declaration, yet (dir my.task) does not list it, so an agent cannot find how to file a finding as a task and root wrote task files by hand. Done when my.task has a thin create! (or a name grounded in the API) over `seon.task/create!` with the agent's world supplied, listed by (dir my.task), documented with an example, and a regression creates a task from an agent's evaluation."
             :must-read #{my.task/open my.task/plan! seon.task/create!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "task-writes-are-untimed-and-refusals-are-hand-built"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Time `my.task/plan!` and done! writes against the 50 ms hot-path bound (R1), and convert the refusals in task.clj and my/task.clj to `seon.error.refusal/error` against declared alternatives. Also reported by landing-reds-2: `my.task/plan!` returned {} for a fresh agent on its own branch, and a task-steps compaction test saw root compact! return {} (writer refused?) - verify both after c135d9993/c99c71dfd (the task-home write path from a branch). Done when writes are timed on the live path with numbers, the refusals are the one error shape, and each {} answer is reproduced or refuted with a regression."
             :must-read #{my.task/done! my.task/plan! seon.task/task-steps seon.turn/compact!}}
 #:seon.task{:name "open-offers-no-authored-tasks",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`my.task/open` answers only tasks with `:seon.task/faults` or `:seon.task/signal`. Faults no longer create tasks (owner 2026-10-07), and nothing writes signal tasks, so the queue no longer offers any new work: tasks filed with `seon.task/create!` or ingested from resources/seon/tasks never appear, though the lane rules tell agents to search it. Correct: the queue is the open, unassigned tasks (`seon.task/open-tasks`), whatever created them. Proof: a task created with `seon.task/create!` appears in (`my.task/open` db).",
            :must-read #{my.task/open}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "open-takes-over-a-second-validating-query-variables",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "(`my.task/open` (`seon.db/db`)) on default took 1,447 ms with 13,748 armed calls, led by `seon.schema.datahike/query-variable?` 6,004 calls, `seon.db/database-value?` 1,285 and `seon.db/query-binding-values` 414, and returned 12 tasks. The second identical call took 54 ms, so the cost is per-acquisition work proportional to queries/schema rather than to the 12 tasks returned. Correct: listing open tasks is sub-second cold; find what query-variable? is called 6,004 times on."}]
